From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gaurav Suman <gauravsuman007@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:34:43 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: align kernel-managed GPU VAs to 64 KiB

On an Adreno 640 (SM8150, LG G8x) GNOME Shell triggers SMMU
translation faults with source=CCU, about ten per login, when
freedreno uses GMEM (binning) rendering. FD_MESA_DEBUG=nogmem or
=inorder hides them.

A stall-on-fault capture decoded with crashdec shows the faulting
address at an exact 64 KiB boundary (FAR 0x110690000) while the pass
drew to MRT0 at 0x110695000 and depth at 0x11069f000, all in that
64 KiB block. The block start belonged to the unmapped tail of
another buffer that was not part of the running submit. A ring
buffer of pagetable operations confirmed this for every fault: the
CCU reads the 64 KiB-aligned start of the block holding a bound
render target, and msm packs kernel-managed VAs at 4 KiB, so that
address often belongs to a different, unmapped buffer. Batch
reordering makes this common, which is why inorder hides it.

Align kernel-managed VAs to 64 KiB, falling back to page alignment
when the range is too tight (a6xx_gmu_init() fails with -ENOSPC
otherwise).

Tested on the LG G8x with Mesa unchanged and GMEM enabled: in an A/B
run in one boot, 4 KiB alignment gave 10 and 10 faults per login, 64
KiB alignment 0, 0, 0 and 0. No faults in three further logins, a
GMEM stress test or the full device test suite; glmark2 is unchanged
within noise. Userspace-managed VMs (VM_BIND) are not changed; Mesa
may want the same alignment there.

This was investigated, written and tested with the help of an AI
coding assistant (Claude Opus 5.5, Anthropic); I reviewed the change
and the test results.

Signed-off-by: Gaurav Suman <gauravsuman007@gmail.com>
Nura-Category: experimental
Nura-Origin: freedreno list 2026-11-09 msg48724
Upstream-Status: proposed, questioned by maintainer
---
Original report and patch:
  https://www.mail-archive.com/freedreno@lists.freedesktop.org/msg48724.html
Maintainer reply (Rob Clark, 2026-11-09, msg48725): "This seems like it is
papering over a userspace bug..", asking for a Mesa issue with a GPU
devcoredump attached. No revised version was posted. This recipe carries it
as an experiment, not as an upstream fix.

Adaptation for v7.0.0-sm8150 (nura rc4j):
 * The change is the msg48724 hunk: their comment verbatim, SZ_64K instead of
   PAGE_SIZE in the first insert, and the -ENOSPC page-aligned retry.  The
   only difference is whitespace: the retry's continuation lines use the
   file's tab indentation instead of aligning under the opening parenthesis.
   The hunk sits at line 382 here instead of 388; nothing else in the file is
   touched.
 * No size gate: every kernel-managed object is 64 KiB aligned, as proposed.
   A size >= SZ_64K test would leave sub-64 KiB buffers able to own a block
   start again, which is the case the alignment is meant to remove. The
   padding is affordable: the managed "gpu" VM is ADRENO_VM_START (0x100000000)
   to BIT(ias), i.e. at least 4 GiB on this board.
 * The PAGE_SIZE retry is the only fallback and it is complete: with a range
   argument, drm_mm_insert_node_in_range() returns 0 or -ENOSPC, nothing else.
 * GMU allocations do share this path (gmu->vm is managed), so the fallback
   covers them. a6xx_gmu_memory_alloc() fixes some windows exactly:
     dummy   [0x60000000, 0x60001000) size 0x1000   - 64 KiB impossible
     icache  [0x00004000, 0x00040000) size 0x3c000  - no 64 KiB start fits
     dcache  [0x00044000, 0x00080000) size 0x3c000  - no 64 KiB start fits
   Those three fail the aligned insert with -ENOSPC and take the page-aligned
   retry, i.e. the current behaviour. The log/HFI booleans are not fixed and
   allocate from [0x60001000, 0x80000000), so they simply land 64 KiB aligned.
 * VM_BIND/user-managed VMs are untouched: the change is inside `if
   (vm->managed)`, and a user VM is created with managed=false, for which
   msm_gem_vma_new() never calls drm_mm_insert_node_in_range() at all.
 * Not tested on hardware. Verified here: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` on the
   0001-0072 tree, and msm_gem_vma.o compiles with no diagnostics.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c
index adf88cf..2bb87f2 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c
@@ -382,9 +382,20 @@ msm_gem_vma_new(struct drm_gpuvm *gpuvm, struct drm_gem_object *obj,
 	if (vm->managed) {
 		BUG_ON(offset != 0);
 		BUG_ON(!obj);  /* NULL mappings not valid for kernel managed VM */
+		/*
+		 * On a6xx (seen on A640) the CCU can read the 64 KiB-aligned
+		 * start of the block that holds a bound render target. Align
+		 * kernel-managed VAs to 64 KiB so that block start lies inside
+		 * the buffer itself; callers with a tight fixed window, such as
+		 * the GMU, fall back to page alignment.
+		 */
 		ret = drm_mm_insert_node_in_range(&vm->mm, &vma->node,
-						obj->size, PAGE_SIZE, 0,
+						obj->size, SZ_64K, 0,
 						range_start, range_end, 0);
+		if (ret == -ENOSPC)
+			ret = drm_mm_insert_node_in_range(&vm->mm, &vma->node,
+							obj->size, PAGE_SIZE, 0,
+							range_start, range_end, 0);
 
 		if (ret)
 			goto err_free_vma;
